\subsection{SDF Label Computation}
\label{app:sdf_labels}

Ground-truth signed distance labels are precomputed offline from LIBERO demonstration data. For each demonstration timestep, we replay the recorded robot state in MuJoCo and compute the minimum signed distance between the end-effector collision geometries (gripper fingers and the last two arm links) and all scene obstacle geometries using \texttt{mj\_geomDistance} for exact signed distance queries.

\paragraph{Grasp exclusion.} Objects currently grasped by the robot are excluded from signed distance computation to avoid penalizing intentional contact during manipulation. Grasp is detected when both conditions are met: (1) gripper aperture falls below 35\,mm, and (2) the minimum finger-to-object distance is less than 5\,mm. When a grasp is detected, the grasped object is removed from the obstacle set $\mathcal{O}$ for that timestep.

\paragraph{Label storage.} Per-timestep signed distances are stored as compressed NumPy arrays indexed by (task\_index, episode\_index, timestep). At training time, the \texttt{SDFLabelStore} aligns labels to each sampled action chunk. When an action chunk extends beyond the demonstration length, the last observed signed distance value is used for padding. The \texttt{SDFAugmentedDataset} wrapper marks samples without matching SDF labels as invalid, excluding them from the SDF loss computation while leaving the flow-matching loss unaffected.

\paragraph{Coverage.} SDF labels cover 57 tasks across 8 task suites. The four standard LIBERO suites have full coverage (10/10 tasks each, 40 tasks total), while the four SafeLIBERO suites have partial coverage (4--5 tasks each, 17 tasks total). Each task provides 50 demonstrations with per-timestep annotations.


\section{SafeLIBERO Benchmark Details}
\label{app:safelibero}

\subsection{Benchmark Structure}

SafeLIBERO \citep{hu2025aegis} extends the standard LIBERO benchmark by introducing collision-relevant obstacles into tabletop manipulation scenes. The benchmark comprises four suites, each containing 4 tasks:

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Spatial} (4 tasks). Obstacles are placed at various locations in the workspace, testing the robot's ability to navigate around static obstructions while performing spatial rearrangement tasks.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Object} (4 tasks). Obstacles are placed on or near the target objects, testing the robot's ability to interact with objects in cluttered proximity.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Goal} (4 tasks). Obstacles are placed at or near the goal locations, testing the robot's ability to place objects in constrained target regions.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Long} (4 tasks). Multi-step manipulation tasks with obstacles present throughout the workspace, testing collision avoidance over extended horizons.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Trials}: 5 evaluation trials per task per method.
\item \textbf{Seeds}: Results in the current version report seed=1. We target 3 random seeds per configuration for the final submission.
\item \textbf{Metrics}: TSR, CAR, and $\mathrm{SS} = \mathrm{TSR} \times \mathrm{CAR}$.
\item \textbf{Policy serving}: Models are served via \texttt{openpi/scripts/serve\_policy.py} using the \texttt{pi05\_libero} configuration. \ours{} uses the same serving command; the SDF head is not loaded at inference time.
\end{itemize}


